  "final_answer": "## Finding: an event was created 82 minutes after Pending Close\n\nThe supplied records identify the September order ending in **1070** as **MO-25-09-1070**, for a Cooler Plate Pack at Modine’s Racine operation.\n\n| Audit item | Recorded value |\n|---|---|\n| Work order ID | `WO-2025-09-004` |\n| Manufacturing order number | `MO-25-09-1070` |\n| Source transaction | `TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070` |\n| Cost event | `CE-250904-0004` — `INPUT` / component issue |\n| Event amount | **USD $214,698.34** — 21,469,834 cents |\n| Event-created time | **`2025-09-30T19:22:00`** |\n| September Pending Close time | **`2025-09-30T18:00:00`** |\n| Time after Pending Close | **82 minutes — 1 hour 22 minutes** |\n| Recorded accounting status | **`POSTED`** |\n\nThe calculation is `19:22:00 − 18:00:00 = 4,920 seconds ÷ 60 = 82 minutes`. Both timestamps are reproduced exactly; the source does not specify a time zone.\n\n### Is the recorded status consistent with Oracle’s policy?\n\n**No.** Oracle’s *Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting* documentation states that new transactions falling in or before a Pending Close period are not staged in Cost Accounting or Receipt Accounting and are processed after the period is closed or reopened. Transactions created after Pending Close are not processed, except for revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments.\n\nThis event is a newly created **INPUT/component issue**, not one of those exceptions. Its **`POSTED`** status is therefore **inconsistent with the documented Pending Close treatment** on the supplied facts.\n\n**Sources:** `work_orders`, `work_order_cost_events`, and `cost_periods` records from the supplied Oracle dataset; Oracle SCM Cost Management 26B policy excerpt, PDF page 233 / printed page 225.\n\nThe review was read-only; **no database records were changed**. The audit result and provenance were saved to `/cache/work/worker_da2c22f2570f/audit-report.json`.",
